\par\par \b \uc2  Romania's Leu Seen Facing Winter Test In  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Markets \b0\par\par\uc2  By Adam Brown \par \uc2  Staff Reporter \par \uc2 711 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 1997\par 17:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUCHAREST -(Dow Jones)- Winter purchases of oil, although lower than in 1996, may cause Romania's leu to depreciate more quickly over the coming months, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The leu's weakness may be compounded by the removal of regulations limiting individuals from purchasing more than $500 in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although these pressures may be balanced by a long-expected - but yet to materialize - inflow of foreign investment to the country's securities markets. \par \par \uc2  The first hint of greater volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets came last week, when the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was liberalized in March, the National Bank has allowed the leu to depreciate in line with inflation, while boosting its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves by buying dollars on an almost daily basis. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts doubt the National Bank will be prepared to use its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to support the leu if it does begin to fall more quickly. \par \par \uc2  Parvoleta Shtereva, an analyst with ING Barings in London, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  acted rapidly to halt the leu's slide last week, but doubted whether it will do the same in the near future. \par \par \uc2  "I still don't believe they'll be prepared to do this on a daily basis," said Shtereva. "They certainly don't want to bleed reserves all through the fourth quarter." \par \par \uc2  Shtereva said that with reserves at about $2.5 billion the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the means, if not the will, to stabilize the leu. \par \par \uc2  But she said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be reluctant to part with reserves that it has raised from $700 million in January. \par \par \uc2  Shtereva predicts the leu will reach 8,200 per dollar by the end of the year, while Paul Dinescu, treasury director of ABN Amro in Bucharest, believes the leu will end the year at about 8,000 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  The leu settled Monday at about 7,600 to the dollar, after the dollar rose to as high as 7,720 lei last Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Traders say oil imports may be the most important factor in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market until energy demand subsides early next year. They say state oil importer Petrol Export-Import has already bought $100 million in September, and will likely buy another $50 million in coming weeks before stepping up purchases again in November. \par \par \uc2  Oil imports are expected to be lower than the 3.2 million tons purchased in 1996, reflecting an expected 2% decline in gross domestic product from last year. \par \par \uc2  However, there may be additional pressure on the leu beginning in October, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  enacts legislation to allow Romanian citizens to buy as much hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they want. Romanians can currently buy a maximum of $500 per year but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it will change the legislation to adopt International Monetary Fund standards on internal \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  convertibility. \par \par \uc2  Traders say private Romanians, with an average monthly wage of about $80, can't buy enough dollars to have a medium-term effect on the leu. But private \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  bureaus may want to stock up on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in advance of the new legislation, so there could be a short-term impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , say traders. \par \par \uc2  Romania's fledgling capital markets are the least predictable factor and could force the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect the leu from either steep depreciation or appreciation. \par \par \uc2  Romanian stock brokers have been predicting the arrival of a flood of foreign investment ever since the government began to remove restrictions on the repatriation of foreign investments in July. \par \par \uc2  Such a flood would boost the leu, and force the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain the near-daily dollar purchases it has conducted over the last six months. \par \par \uc2  But if anything, foreign investment appears to be slowing down. During a stock market rally in June and early July, foreign investment pushed daily trading volumes up to more than 20 billion lei. But despite the listing of about 20 new shares since then, trading volume now averages about eight billion lei per day as domestic investors regain some market dominance and shares prices drop. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1997 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  1106 1838 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020011006dt9t00rfk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
